"""Original adrenal chapter: deficiency, replacement and congenital steroid pathways."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
AI=ref('Endocrine Society: Primary Adrenal Insufficiency, 2016','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880116/')
GC=ref('ESE/Endocrine Society: Glucocorticoid-Induced Adrenal Insufficiency, 2024','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11180513/')
EC=ref('Society for Endocrinology: Adrenal Crisis Guidance','https://www.endocrinology.org/clinical-practice/clinical-guidance/adrenal-crisis/')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('Weight loss, orthostatic hypotension, hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia and hyperkalemia accompany low cortisol. Which pattern most strongly supports primary adrenal insufficiency?', 'Markedly raised ACTH with evidence of mineralocorticoid deficiency',[
 ('Low ACTH with preserved aldosterone','That favors central or glucocorticoid-induced deficiency.'),('Suppressed renin and high aldosterone','That suggests aldosterone excess rather than deficiency.'),('High cortisol with low ACTH','That suggests an ACTH-independent cortisol-excess mechanism.')],
 'Primary failure removes both cortisol feedback and often aldosterone action', '**ACTH rises while cortisol falls, and renin can rise as sodium is lost.**',['ACTH-related melanocortin stimulation contributes to pigmentation.','Not every feature is present at diagnosis.'], '**Confirm adrenal reserve when safe and identify the cause.**',['Treat a suspected crisis immediately.','Autoimmune testing and context-directed imaging refine etiology.'],[['Cortisol','Low'],['ACTH','High'],['Renin/aldosterone','High renin with inadequate aldosterone']],[AI],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with known Addison disease has vomiting, hypotension and confusion during gastroenteritis. What is the immediate treatment priority?', 'Parenteral hydrocortisone and appropriate fluid resuscitation without waiting for confirmatory testing',[
 ('Continue oral tablets only despite persistent vomiting','Absorption and retention are unreliable.'),('Wait for a stimulation test before treatment','This delays lifesaving therapy.'),('Treat with fludrocortisone alone','The crisis requires glucocorticoid replacement and circulatory support.')],
 'A known replacement requirement becomes an emergency when oral delivery fails', '**Adult crisis treatment commonly begins with 100 mg hydrocortisone IV or IM.**',['Ongoing therapy is usually 200 mg over 24 hours or 50 mg every six hours.','Fluid treatment is tailored to circulation and cardiac/renal reserve.'], '**Treat hypoglycemia and the precipitating illness as needed.**',['Monitor electrolytes and clinical response.','Obtain samples first only if they do not delay rescue.'],[['Crisis','Prompt parenteral steroid'],['Circulation','Appropriate isotonic fluid'],['Cause','Treat illness and restore reliable coverage']],[EC,AI],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which maintenance regimen best reflects physiological glucocorticoid replacement for many adults with primary adrenal insufficiency?', 'Hydrocortisone in divided doses, with the largest dose in the morning and an individualized total commonly 15–25 mg/day',[
 ('A large supraphysiological dose every night for all patients','This poorly mimics physiology and increases overreplacement risk.'),('Glucocorticoids only when symptoms become severe','Regular replacement is required in established deficiency.'),('A fixed dose adjusted solely to normalize ACTH regardless of clinical effects','ACTH normalization can drive overtreatment.')],
 'Replacement seeks adequate coverage without chronic excess', '**The normal cortisol rhythm is highest around waking.**',['Dose distribution and total need vary.','Modified-release or alternative regimens can suit selected patients.'], '**Assess clinical wellbeing and signs of excess or deficiency.**',['Weight, blood pressure, energy and metabolic effects matter.','Do not use a persistently high ACTH alone to justify escalating indefinitely.'],[['Common total','15–25 mg/day'],['Timing','Largest dose on waking'],['Monitoring','Clinical adequacy and toxicity']],[AI])
q('Which findings help guide fludrocortisone replacement in primary adrenal insufficiency?', 'Postural symptoms, blood pressure, sodium, potassium and renin in context',[
 ('A1C alone','It does not assess mineralocorticoid replacement.'),('ACTH alone as a direct aldosterone marker','ACTH is not the main controller of mineralocorticoid balance.'),('A fixed dose that is never reassessed despite edema or hypotension','Clinical needs can change.')],
 'Mineralocorticoid replacement targets sodium and volume balance', '**Underreplacement can cause salt craving, postural symptoms and raised renin.**',['Overreplacement can cause hypertension or edema.','Heat, sweating and interacting drugs can change requirements.'], '**Review the whole balance rather than a single laboratory number.**',['Do not impose routine salt restriction in a salt-losing replacement state without context.','High-dose stress hydrocortisone has mineralocorticoid activity, affecting acute management.'],[['Underreplacement','Postural symptoms/salt craving'],['Overreplacement','Hypertension/edema'],['Monitoring','Clinical balance plus electrolytes/renin']],[AI])
q('A patient with autoimmune adrenal insufficiency is otherwise stable. Which associated condition merits awareness and screening according to clinical context?', 'Autoimmune thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes as part of autoimmune clustering',[
 ('Only nonautoimmune disorders can coexist','Autoimmune endocrine disorders cluster.'),('Normal potassium excludes all associated disease','Potassium does not determine autoimmune risk.'),('Every affected patient must have all syndrome components at diagnosis','Associated diseases can appear at different times.')],
 'Autoimmune polyglandular disease can evolve over time', '**Thyroid disease, diabetes, pernicious anemia and other autoimmune conditions may coexist.**',['Twenty-one-hydroxylase antibodies support autoimmune adrenal etiology.','Age, family history and phenotype guide broader investigation.'], '**Maintain longitudinal surveillance.**',['Explain new symptom triggers.','Ensure adrenal coverage before introducing thyroid replacement in a newly recognized combined deficiency.'],[['Etiology clue','21-hydroxylase antibodies'],['Common clustering','Thyroid disease and type 1 diabetes'],['Course','Can evolve over years']],[AI])
q('Primary adrenal insufficiency occurs with bilateral adrenal enlargement in a patient with relevant infection exposure. What is the appropriate etiological principle?', 'Investigate infiltrative and infectious causes such as tuberculosis rather than assuming every case is autoimmune',[
 ('Adrenal imaging alone identifies every organism','Microbiological and clinical evidence are needed.'),('All bilateral enlargement is necessarily metastatic cancer','The differential includes infection and other infiltrative processes.'),('Cause evaluation can replace urgent steroid treatment in an unstable patient','Stabilization must not be delayed.')],
 'The anatomical and epidemiological pattern refines the differential', '**Tuberculosis and fungal disease can involve both glands.**',['Hemorrhage, metastases and infiltrative disease are additional causes.','Chronic infection can later produce calcification or atrophy.'], '**Combine replacement with cause-directed care.**',['Review imaging, exposure and targeted tests.','Treatment interactions can alter steroid requirements.'],[['Pattern','Bilateral adrenal involvement'],['Differential','Infection, infiltration or hemorrhage'],['Priority','Replacement plus etiological treatment']],[AI],'clinical_scenario')
q('Why can rifampicin precipitate underreplacement in a patient taking hydrocortisone?', 'Enzyme induction increases glucocorticoid metabolism',[
 ('It directly supplies cortisol','It does not provide hormone replacement.'),('It always blocks cortisol metabolism','Induction increases clearance.'),('All glucocorticoid doses remain adequate despite interacting drugs','Exposure can change substantially.')],
 'A replacement dose is vulnerable to pharmacokinetic change', '**Enzyme-inducing medicines can increase steroid requirements.**',['Interacting treatment can also complicate diagnostic tests.','The effect depends on the medicine and clinical context.'], '**Review symptoms and coverage when the interacting agent starts or stops.**',['Use specialist dose planning rather than waiting for crisis.','Provide stress-dose and emergency instructions.'],[['Interaction','Enzyme induction'],['Effect','Faster glucocorticoid clearance'],['Risk','Underreplacement']],[AI,GC])
q('Long-term prednisolone is no longer needed for the underlying inflammatory disease. Which taper principle is appropriate?', 'Reduce more quickly through supraphysiological doses, then more cautiously near physiological exposure with recovery assessment when appropriate',[
 ('Stop every long course abruptly','HPA suppression and disease relapse can make this unsafe.'),('Test adrenal function routinely while high-dose therapy is still required','The 2024 guideline generally advises against unnecessary testing in that setting.'),('The same taper fits every disease, dose and duration','Underlying disease and exposure determine the plan.')],
 'Two risks coexist during withdrawal', '**The original disease can relapse, and the suppressed HPA axis may not yet provide adequate cortisol.**',['Withdrawal symptoms can overlap with adrenal insufficiency.','Testing becomes relevant near physiological dosing when discontinuation is planned.'], '**Use a documented taper and illness plan.**',['Slow or reassess when significant symptoms occur.','Do not leave the patient without instructions for vomiting or major stress.'],[['Higher doses','Underlying disease guides reduction'],['Near physiology','Slower taper/recovery assessment'],['Safety','Stress-dose education']],[GC])
q('A patient near physiological dosing after a long steroid course has morning cortisol above 10 µg/dL under an appropriate testing protocol. What does the 2024 guideline suggest?', 'HPA recovery is likely enough to allow stopping glucocorticoid replacement in the appropriate clinical context',[
 ('The result proves all future illness can never require reassessment','Clinical context and uncertainty still matter.'),('All values above 3 prove complete recovery under every assay','The guideline uses a higher practical recovery threshold.'),('A result above 10 mandates lifelong higher-dose steroids','It generally supports recovery rather than continued excess.')],
 'Morning cortisol is interpreted as a continuum', '**The 2024 guideline uses above 10 µg/dL as a practical recovery guide.**',['Values around 5–10 usually lead to continued physiological coverage and later retesting.','Low values support continued coverage with reassessment.'], '**Use correct timing and account for prior glucocorticoid interference.**',['Thresholds depend on assay and clinical setting.','This approach is not the same as every diagnostic pathway for new primary disease.'],[['Above 10','Recovery likely'],['5–10','Continue and retest'],['Below 5','Recovery less likely']],[GC],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient uses inhaled fluticasone and starts a potent CYP3A inhibitor, then develops Cushingoid features and a suppressed cortisol axis. What is the key mechanism?', 'Increased systemic glucocorticoid exposure despite a nonoral route',[
 ('Inhaled or topical steroids can never suppress the HPA axis','Systemic absorption and interactions can produce suppression.'),('Cushingoid features exclude glucocorticoid-induced adrenal insufficiency','Exogenous excess and suppressed endogenous reserve can coexist.'),('A low endogenous cortisol proves there was no glucocorticoid exposure','Exogenous steroid can suppress the measured endogenous hormone.')],
 'Route does not eliminate systemic interaction risk', '**Strong metabolism inhibitors can amplify exposure to certain inhaled or topical glucocorticoids.**',['Multiple formulations and prolonged high potency increase risk.','Abrupt removal of exposure can reveal adrenal insufficiency.'], '**Review the complete medicine list and replace safely when necessary.**',['Coordinate alternatives and tapering.','Provide stress coverage until recovery is established.'],[['Interaction','Reduced glucocorticoid metabolism'],['Appearance','Exogenous Cushingoid phenotype'],['Endogenous axis','Can be suppressed']],[GC],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with adrenal insufficiency develops fever but can retain oral medication. What is a key preventive action?', 'Follow the prescribed sick-day increase in glucocorticoid coverage and seek help if oral intake or clinical status deteriorates',[
 ('Keep the same dose during every major illness','Stress can increase cortisol needs.'),('Increase only fludrocortisone while ignoring glucocorticoids','The principal acute stress requirement is glucocorticoid.'),('Delay emergency injection until prolonged shock develops','Vomiting or severe illness can require earlier parenteral treatment.')],
 'Education prevents a foreseeable replacement failure', '**Patients need written illness rules and an emergency steroid plan.**',['Medical alert identification and an injection kit can be lifesaving.','The exact increase follows individual and local guidance.'], '**Teach caregivers as well as the patient.**',['Persistent vomiting or inability to retain tablets changes the route.','Reassess after recovery to return to maintenance safely.'],[['Can retain tablets','Use agreed oral sick-day plan'],['Cannot retain/severe illness','Parenteral rescue and urgent care'],['Prevention','Education, kit and alert identification']],[AI,EC])
q('Virilization and salt-wasting adrenal insufficiency occur with markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone. Which enzyme defect is most likely?', '21-hydroxylase deficiency',[
 ('11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency as the typical salt-wasting pattern','That more often causes deoxycorticosterone-mediated hypertension.'),('17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency','This typically reduces sex steroid production and causes hypertension.'),('Aromatase deficiency as the direct cortisol and aldosterone block','It does not create the same steroidogenic pattern.')],
 'A steroid precursor pattern localizes the enzymatic block', '**Reduced cortisol raises ACTH, driving adrenal hyperplasia and androgen production.**',['Severe forms also impair aldosterone synthesis.','Nonclassic disease can present later without salt wasting.'], '**Confirm using the appropriate biochemical and genetic pathway.**',['Treat adrenal crisis promptly.','Long-term management balances replacement against androgen control and overtreatment.'],[['Marker','17-hydroxyprogesterone'],['Androgens','Increased'],['Severe form','Salt wasting']],[AI])
q('Congenital adrenal hyperplasia causes androgen excess with hypertension and low renin. Which accumulated steroid particularly suggests 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?', 'Deoxycorticosterone',[
 ('Aldosterone as the necessary high hormone','Renin and aldosterone are often suppressed by mineralocorticoid-like precursor activity.'),('Only 17-hydroxyprogesterone without other pathway data','That marker is especially used for 21-hydroxylase disease and is not the full distinguishing mechanism.'),('Calcitriol','This is not an adrenal steroid precursor.')],
 'A precursor can have mineralocorticoid activity', '**DOC accumulation causes sodium retention and hypertension.**',['Cortisol deficiency drives ACTH and androgen excess.','This contrasts with salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase disease.'], '**Use the steroid profile and phenotype together.**',['Confirm the specific pathway.','Treat glucocorticoid deficiency and blood-pressure consequences under specialist care.'],[['11-beta defect','DOC excess'],['Blood pressure','Often high'],['Androgens','Often elevated']],[AI])
q('After successful removal of an autonomous cortisol-secreting adrenal tumor, why may temporary glucocorticoid replacement be required?', 'Chronic cortisol excess suppressed the remaining HPA axis',[
 ('All unilateral adrenal operations permanently remove both glands','The other gland remains but may be functionally suppressed.'),('A normal-looking contralateral gland guarantees immediate full reserve','Recovery can take time.'),('Steroids are needed only if the original tumor was malignant','Suppression depends on hormone exposure rather than malignancy alone.')],
 'Removing excess does not instantly restore endogenous regulation', '**ACTH suppression can reduce contralateral adrenal responsiveness.**',['Withdrawal symptoms and true adrenal insufficiency can overlap.','Recovery duration varies.'], '**Use planned replacement, taper and reassessment.**',['Provide stress-dose instructions during recovery.','Avoid declaring recovery from anatomy alone.'],[['Before surgery','Autonomous cortisol excess'],['After removal','Possible suppressed reserve'],['Plan','Temporary coverage and recovery testing']],[AI,GC])
